{
  "dom_id": "step-q315939",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274523/assets/fb1e438fd323fdb14a40d880d3691d94ccac374d43d054d85b6a7e2e5023cbe1.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $MON$ with area $A=100$ square units. Determine the angle $\\theta $ (in radians) of the circular sector that has the smallest possible perimeter. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}  radians",
  "worked_solution": "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $100$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{200}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{200}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{200}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{200}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{200}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =200 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =100 \\\\ r & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we confirm that $r=10$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{400}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=10$ is a minimum. \n\nFinally, when $r=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{200}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{200}{100} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
